#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Launch an Ask AI observer: attach to the latest game with one AI agent
# selected, and answer "what would it do here" from the game screen.
set -eo pipefail

if [ $# -ge 1 ] && { [ "$1" = "-h" ] || [ "$1" = "--help" ]; }; then
  cat <<'EOF'
Usage: bin/observe [--agent <spec>] [--new] [--lobby <url>] [--once]

Attaches to the most recently launched game as an *observer*: it takes no
seat and never plays. While it is attached, the game screen shows an
"Ask AI" icon; clicking it asks this agent what it would do in the
position on screen, and prints the same explanation here.

Options:
  --agent <spec>  Which agent explains. Any sim registry spec:
                    h2                     modular AI (default) — richest
                                           explanation, module by module
                    heuristic              the rule-based weights
                    'mc:ms=2000/turns=2'   Monte-Carlo search, 2s per answer
                    'bc:weights.json'      a trained policy
  --new           Wait for the NEXT game instead of the one already running.
                  Start the observer first, then click Play.
  --lobby <url>   Lobby base URL (default http://localhost:8080).
  --once          Explain the newest recorded position, print it, and exit.
                  No browser needed.
  -h, --help      Show this message.

The observer reads positions from the game log under ~/.meccg/logs/games,
so it must run on the same machine as the game server, and it authenticates
to the lobby with MASTER_KEY (or ~/.meccg/secrets.json).

One process follows game after game: when a game ends it waits for the next.

Examples:
  bin/observe                          # explain with h2, attach to the live game
  bin/observe --new --agent heuristic  # start first, then launch the game
  bin/observe --agent 'mc:ms=2000' --once
EOF
  exit 0
fi

cd "$(dirname "$0")/.."
# tsconfig.dev.json maps the workspace packages onto their source: an observer
# resolving the built dist would explain the last `npm run build`, not the agent
# code in the working tree.
exec npx tsx --tsconfig packages/text-client/tsconfig.dev.json \
  packages/text-client/src/observer-client.ts "$@"
